"""Сообщения (Messages): an unofficial client for the MAX messenger (and
Telegram, through Telethon: TelethonLink), no web browser, part of the
Haltia desktop.

io.github.haltia_de.Messenger: a desktop program of its own, on any GTK 3
desktop; the Haltia shell meets it only through freedesktop standards (its
.desktop file, the Unity LauncherEntry count, MPRIS, notifications, XDG
autostart) and gives the look through its GTK theme. Shared code is the
kit's (haltia_kit 2: settings, keeping awake, photos, moving folders). Until 2.0 it was b8k-max, io.github.trudenboy.Max; its files move
over at the first start (move_old_home).

PyMax (MIT, unpacked in PREFIX/lib/haltia-messenger) speaks MAX's web
protocol over a WebSocket, as web.max.ru does, so no web engine is loaded:
about 55 MB in all instead of a second browser. The first start shows a QR
code to scan in MAX on a phone; the session is then kept in the keyring
(the Secret Service; where there is none, ~/.local/share/haltia-messenger/
max-session.json, mode 600). PyMax's simulated telemetry is
off: a person is typing here.

Telemetry of our own instead, as MAX's app reports it, so that the server
sees an ordinary client and is less likely to block the account: the
screens a person really goes through (to the list, into a chat, the
window hidden) and how long a chat took to show, in the event shapes of
PyMax 2.4.1. No texts, no names. It is on until switched off in the
settings (the gear in the header), kept in ~/.config/haltia/messenger.conf with
the notifications of new messages.

Pages: the chat list (initials, name, last message, time, unread count)
and a chat (bubbles, photos, a large entry and a send button). New
messages arrive live; a new one in a loud chat is a desktop notification
(the desktop buzzes or sounds for it), unless switched off in the settings.
The search (the magnifier over the list) finds a person by phone number,
to write to or add to the contacts, a channel or group by its max.ru link,
to join, or one of the own chats by name. The edit button in a chat's
header opens its menu: rename the person, pin, notifications, and delete
(a dialog, for this account only) or leave (a group, a channel), after a
question on the page. One thing at a time, by hand: nothing here searches
or adds in bulk.

A long press on a message offers, beside the reactions, to forward it
(MAX forwards it with its attachments; a forwarded one says from whom) and
to save what it carries into a folder (Загрузки/MAX unless set otherwise).

MAX has no setting for who may write to an account, so by default the
dialogs of people outside the contacts are kept off the list, the badge
and the notifications here (the settings switch them back). Known are the contacts
MAX sends at login, those added here, those written to here, and, the
first time, everyone already in a dialog; the ids are kept in
~/.local/share/haltia-messenger/known.json (mode 600), since after the first login
MAX sends only the contacts that changed. Who may add the account to chats
is a setting of MAX itself: shown as MAX sends it at login, changed only in
MAX's own app (it refuses web clients that, "safe mode").
PyMax runs in its own thread with an asyncio loop; every call from the
window goes through run(), every answer comes back with GLib.idle_add.

It lives in the background: XDG autostart starts it with --background
with the session (with no graphical session it exits at once), closing
the window only hides it, and starting it again brings the same process
back (one instance through D-Bus). The unread count of loud chats goes to
its launcher button as Unity's LauncherEntry. Chats open from a cache: the last
messages of each chat on disk (~/.cache/haltia-messenger/chats, mode 600) and the
last six chats kept built in memory; a chat with nothing new since its
cache opens without asking MAX at all. The list keeps its rows and redraws
only a chat that changed. Photos are cached on disk and pruned when not
shown for a month (daily, as the program runs for weeks).

The list has two tabs, chats and channels. A long press on a chat pins it
to the top or switches its notifications (and its part of the count);
channels are quiet unless switched on. Both are kept here
(~/.local/share/haltia-messenger/chats.json): PyMax has no pinning or muting.

A chat opens with its cache and the newest PAGE messages; two older pages
follow in the background, the rest as the reader scrolls up, keeping the
place. Only the newest FIRST messages are built at once (a screen of them);
every other bubble is built a few at a time when the window is idle, so
scrolling and going back never wait for a page to be laid out; a spinner
shows while an older page comes. A chat left keeps only its newest BUBBLES
built and, back in it, the place it was left at; photos are fetched only
near the screen. All of that is ChatView's, one per chat (CONTEXT.md).
Voice messages play in the bubble (libmpv,
sound only); a video opens full screen in haltia-vplay (mpv elsewhere) and a
file in the browser, by the short-lived link MAX gives on request.

Reactions: a long press on a message offers six emoji; those on it show
under it with their counts, the own one highlighted, a tap puts one on or
takes the own off. Others' reactions arrive live.

The person of a dialog can be renamed (the edit menu): here only, kept in
~/.local/share/haltia-messenger/names.json, since MAX's web protocol as PyMax has it
offers no renaming of contacts.
"""
